Determine the number of ways to place $N$ elephants on a $2 \times n$ chessboard such that no two elephants attack each other. Since an elephant (bishop) can only attack squares on the same diagonal, we can observe that we must place the elephants in separate columns. This is because if an elephant is placed in one column, it can only attack one square of the other diagonal in the same row.

Let us denote the number of ways to place elephants on a $2 \times n$ board as $W(n)$. We will use a greedy algorithm to place the elephants. We start by placing an elephant in the first column, which leaves us with two cells in the second column, and one of these cells will be attacked by the elephant in the first column. Therefore, we can only place one elephant in the second column to make sure that no elephants attack each other.

So, if we place an elephant in the first column, we are left with $W(n-1)$ ways to place the remaining elephants. If we start with an empty first column, then we have to choose between placing one elephant in the second column or two elephants (the second diagonal), which will also leave us with $W(n-1)$ ways. Therefore, we can express $W(n)$ recursively as:

$$ W(n) = W(n-1) + W(n-1) = 2W(n-1) $$

Now, we find the base case. When $n=1$, there is only one cell, so we can place an elephant there in only one way.

Therefore, we can express $W(n)$ using the base case and the recursive formula as:

\begin{align*}
W(1) &= 1 \\
W(n) &= 2W(n-1) \\
&= 2^0W(1) \\
&= 2^0 \\
&= \boxed{1} \\
\end{align*}

